  "dom_id": "step-q72367",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The function $g(p)={e}^{-4{p}^{2}}$ models the volume of gas that a vendor can sell, in liters, at a price of $p$ dollars per liter. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of gas is changing with respect to the price when $p=1.50$ dollars.",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate at which the volume of gas that can be sold is changing when $p=1.50$ dollars is equal to ${g}^{\u2032}(1.5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $g(p)$ with respect to $p$ is \n\n$$\n{g}^{\u2032}(p)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}p}({e}^{-4{p}^{2}})=-8p{e}^{-4{p}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $p=1.5$ gives\n\n$$\n{g}^{\u2032}(1.5)=-8(1.5){e}^{-4(1.5)^{2}}=-12{e}^{-9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of gas that can be sold is changing when $p=1.5$ dollars is $-12{e}^{-9}$ liters per dollar.",
